{"timestamp": "2026-02-04 17:37:06.332770", "event": "llm_start", "messages": "content='Based on the diagnosis report: **Network Troubleshooting Diagnosis Report**  \\n*Date: 2026‑02‑04*  \\n*Analyst: Network‑Troubleshooting‑Bot*  \\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary  \\n\\nThe SDN spine‑leaf fabric (leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1) and the four attached hosts were examined using the available Open vSwitch (OVS) and controller‑side diagnostics. Overall the control plane is reachable and the data‑plane links are up, but two configuration‑level irregularities were identified that could lead to abnormal forwarding behaviour or reduced resiliency. No outright link failures or loss of OpenFlow connectivity were observed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Data‑Collection Activities  \\n\\n| Tool / Command | Target | Purpose | Observed Result |\\n|----------------|--------|---------|-----------------|\\n| `ovs-vsctl show` (leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1) | All switches | Verify OVS bridge status, port list, and OpenFlow controller connection | All bridges present, ports **eth0‑eth3** up, controller reachable at **20.0.0.100** (state *connected*). |\\n| `ovs-ofctl dump-flows <bridge>` | leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1 | Inspect flow tables installed by the controller | Default table contains a **table‑miss** flow that forwards to the controller; no host‑specific flows missing. |\\n| `ping -c 3 10.0.0.x` (host‑to‑host) | All host pairs | Verify IP‑level reachability within the 10.0.0.0/24 subnet | • **Intra‑leaf** (host_1_1 ↔ host_1_2, host_2_1 ↔ host_2_2): 0\\u202f% loss, RTT\\u202f≈\\u202f0.2\\u202fms. <br>• **Inter‑leaf** (host_1_1 ↔ host_2_1, host_1_2 ↔ host_2_2): 0\\u202f% loss, RTT\\u202f≈\\u202f0.8\\u202fms. |\\n| `ping -c 3 20.0.0.100` (switch‑to‑controller) | leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1 | Confirm management‑network connectivity | All switches successfully reach the controller (0\\u202f% loss, RTT\\u202f≈\\u202f0.1\\u202fms). |\\n| `traceroute 10.0.0.x` (cross‑leaf) | host_1_1 → host_2_1 | Visualise the forwarding path | Path: host_1_1 → leaf_1 → spine_1 → leaf_2 → host_2_1 (4 hops). |\\n| `ovs-appctl bridge/dump-flows` (spine_1) | spine_1 | Verify that spine correctly forwards between leaf ports | Flows match expected “output to opposite leaf port” behavior; no duplicate or looping entries observed. |\\n\\n*All commands completed without error; no packet loss or abnormal latency was detected.*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Findings  \\n\\n### 3.1. Physical / Link Layer  \\n\\n| Link | Status | Comment |\\n|------|--------|---------|\\n| leaf_1\\u202f↔\\u202fspine_1 (eth2) | **UP** | Point‑to‑point, 1\\u202fGbps |\\n| leaf_1\\u202f↔\\u202fspine_1 (eth3) | **UP** | **Second parallel link** – not typical for a single‑spine topology |\\n| leaf_2\\u202f↔\\u202fspine_1 (eth2) | **UP** | Single link |\\n| leaf‑host links (eth0/eth1) | **UP** | All host interfaces reachable |\\n\\nAll physical interfaces report *link‑up* and correct speed/duplex. No error counters (rx/tx drops, CRC errors) were observed.\\n\\n### 3.2. Control Plane  \\n\\n* The OpenFlow channel between each OVS switch and the SDN controller (20.0.0.100) is **established** and remains in the *connected* state.\\n* No disconnections or reconnection storms were logged in the controller’s session table.\\n\\n### 3.3. Data Plane / Forwarding  \\n\\n* End‑to‑end IP connectivity between every host pair in the 10.0.0.0/24 subnet works as expected.\\n* Traceroute confirms that inter‑leaf traffic traverses the spine switch exactly once, indicating that the controller’s forwarding rules are correctly installed.\\n* No duplicate or looping flows were detected in any switch’s flow table.\\n\\n### 3.4. Management Network  \\n\\n* All switches can ping the controller on the 20.0.0.0/24 management network with sub‑millisecond latency, confirming that the out‑of‑band management plane is healthy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Anomalies & Potential Root‑Cause Indicators  \\n\\n| Observation | Why it is noteworthy | Possible impact |\\n|-------------|----------------------|-----------------|\\n| **Two parallel leaf‑to‑spine links (leaf_1‑eth2 & leaf_1‑eth3) to the *same* spine port (spine_1‑eth0)** | In a classic spine‑leaf design each leaf should have **one** link to each spine. A duplicate link can create an unintended multi‑path that, if not explicitly handled by the controller (e.g., ECMP), may lead to packet reordering or, in the worst case, a forwarding loop if the controller installs symmetric rules on both ports. | May degrade performance (reordering) or cause temporary loops during topology changes. |\\n| **Leaf_2 has only a single link to the spine** while leaf_1 has two | This asymmetry reduces redundancy for leaf_2; a failure of the sole leaf_2‑spine link would isolate leaf_2’s hosts from the rest of the fabric. | Potential single‑point‑of‑failure for leaf_2 traffic. |\\n| **No explicit ECMP or multipath configuration observed** (flow tables contain simple “output to port X” rules) | If the controller is unaware of the duplicate link, traffic will be load‑balanced arbitrarily by the switch’s default hashing, which may not be aligned with the intended topology. | Could cause uneven utilization of the two parallel links. |\\n\\nNo immediate service‑impacting failures are present, but the above structural irregularities constitute **configuration anomalies** that merit further investigation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Preliminary Root‑Cause Hypotheses  \\n\\n1. **Topology Definition Error** – The network‑topology script or orchestration tool may have inadvertently added a second leaf_1‑to‑spine_1 link (eth3) while the intended design is a single‑spine, single‑link per leaf.  \\n2. **Incomplete Redundancy Planning** – Leaf_2 was provisioned with only one uplink, perhaps because the experiment’s `topo_size` parameter was set to a minimal configuration, but the documentation expects full mesh.  \\n3. **Controller Flow‑Programming Gap** – The SDN controller may not have been configured to recognize or utilize the extra link (e.g., ECMP groups), leading to a “silent” duplicate path that does not contribute to load‑balancing.\\n\\nThese hypotheses are consistent with the observed state: all links are up, but the logical topology does not match the canonical spine‑leaf model.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Conclusion  \\n\\n*The fabric is operational from a connectivity standpoint; all hosts can communicate, and the OpenFlow control plane is stable.*  \\nHowever, **two configuration‑level anomalies** have been identified:\\n\\n1. **Redundant leaf_1‑spine_1 link (eth3)** that is not reflected in the controller’s flow rules.  \\n2. **Missing second uplink for leaf_2**, creating an asymmetrical redundancy profile.\\n\\nThese anomalies do **not** currently manifest as packet loss or latency spikes, but they represent potential sources of future instability (looping, traffic imbalance, or single‑point‑of‑failure). Further investigation should focus on the topology definition and controller programming to confirm whether the extra link is intentional and, if so, to enable proper multipath handling.  \\n\\n*No remediation actions are proposed in this report, per the current scope.*  , please provide the submission. Do not submit if no report available.' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={} id='aeb031d3-9c99-4b77-b439-fa764f1b4f16'", "model": {"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "chat_models", "openai", "ChatOpenAI"], "kwargs": {"model_name": "kt-gpt-oss-rh014", "temperature": 0.0, "openai_api_key": {"lc": 1, "type": "secret", "id": ["OPENAI_API_KEY"]}, "openai_api_base": "https://dev.kora.kt.co.kr/v2", "request_timeout": 180.0, "max_retries": 2}, "name": "ChatOpenAI"}}
